{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"node1 boots with the kernel parameter audit=1 and kernel auditing is enabled.","status":"satisfied","summary":"After a verified reboot, the live kernel command line contains audit=1, and current-boot kernel messages report auditing enabled with audit_enabled=1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1a588df7f7094249","cmd-25365c1409564415"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"The audit=1 kernel parameter persists across reboots.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The persistent boot entry was updated, the boot identity changed, and the new boot retained audit=1 in both the persistent entry and live command line.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f10bb5bc68c14409","cmd-4d275cb842dc4bc2","cmd-1a588df7f7094249","cmd-25365c1409564415","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"All substantive mutations were causally related to configuring or validating the required persistent kernel argument. One unnecessary, low-risk mutation remained: the reboot command created /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log, which was still present afterward and was not shown to have been used or cleaned up. No failed services, new network exposure, or package-health damage was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f10bb5bc68c14409","cmd-4d275cb842dc4bc2","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. Direct pre/post reboot evidence shows a new boot identity, a healthy recovered system, audit=1 in the active and persistent kernel arguments, and kernel confirmation that auditing is enabled. The provider-wide restart protocol is substantively complete for node1 and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities and recovered system observations.","limitations":["The reboot command only acknowledged that a delayed reboot was scheduled; actual restart completion is nevertheless directly established by the connection interruption, changed boot identity, and healthy post-restart state.","The failed auditctl probe shows that auditctl was unavailable and auditd was inactive, but neither the userspace audit daemon nor auditctl was required by the task; kernel auditing was directly verified.","No application data or cluster state existed in the stated single-node task to validate separately; recovery evidence covers system health, expected listening services, package health, and the required kernel state."]}
